# Postern Messenger — LIVE Matrix login / sync / E2EE round-trip evidence — FIRST LIVE RUN
# UNAUDITED Phase-1 reference slice, run end to end against a throwaway local
# Synapse. A passing round-trip is a PLUMBING + E2EE-path proof ONLY. It is NOT
# a security claim and NOT a metadata-privacy claim. The Megolm/Olm crypto is
# CLASSICAL Curve25519 (NOT post-quantum; harvest-now-decrypt-later applies to
# intercepted ciphertext). No Tor is wired on this path — login/sync/send went
# over plain localhost HTTP, so the homeserver saw existence/online-ness, the
# social graph, timing, and the device list (see HOMESERVER_METADATA_LIMITS in
# src/matrix.rs). No cross-signing / SAS verification exists yet (Phase 2). This
# is the first time both #[ignore]d live tests were actually EXECUTED against a
# real homeserver and transcribed — previously the file header's "HONEST STATUS"
# said the restore round-trip was UNPROVEN until someone ran it. It has now run.
#
# GATE (unchanged, never weakened): both tests stay #[ignore]d and require the
# POSTERN_MATRIX_TEST_* env vars to be set to a reachable homeserver. Neither is
# ever a CI gate. The offline `cargo test -p postern-messenger --features matrix`
# suite still lists them as `ignored` and proves nothing beyond compilation.
# The only diff to the test file is the addition of `eprintln!` EVIDENCE lines
# (visible under --nocapture) so the transcript below is self-demonstrating
# rather than "trust the asserts"; no assertion or gate was changed.

host          : Darwin 23.6.0 x86_64 (macOS 14.8.7, BuildVersion 23J520 — dev workstation, NOT CI)
git_rev       : 3bf161faed4e892d27eb8330bf1062f577c410ff (branch base) + this branch's matrix_live evidence prints
toolchain     : rustc 1.94.1 (e408947bf 2026-03-25) / cargo 1.94.1
homeserver    : matrixdotorg/synapse 1.156.0, image digest
                matrixdotorg/synapse@sha256:6882d26594b87171e0fe807ac6bd7f0000665cd70e73fb88c58ec9bff14c19ce
docker        : Docker Desktop server 29.4.0 (local, throwaway container `synapse-live`, volume `synapse-data`)
server_name   : localhost, listening http://localhost:8008 (SYNAPSE_REPORT_STATS=no)
accounts      : alice / test-pass-a and bob / test-pass-b, registered via
                `register_new_matrix_user --no-admin -c /data/homeserver.yaml`
env_vars      : POSTERN_MATRIX_TEST_HOMESERVER=http://localhost:8008
                POSTERN_MATRIX_TEST_USER_A=alice  POSTERN_MATRIX_TEST_PASS_A=test-pass-a
                POSTERN_MATRIX_TEST_USER_B=bob    POSTERN_MATRIX_TEST_PASS_B=test-pass-b
gate          : #[ignore]d AND POSTERN_MATRIX_TEST_* required; never runs in CI. Diff weakens no gate.

# ── UNBLOCK RUNBOOK (exactly what was done on this host) ──
#   1. Started the local Docker daemon (Docker Desktop).
#   2. docker volume create synapse-data
#   3. docker run --rm -v synapse-data:/data -e SYNAPSE_SERVER_NAME=localhost \
#        -e SYNAPSE_REPORT_STATS=no matrixdotorg/synapse:latest generate
#   4. docker run -d --name synapse-live -v synapse-data:/data -p 8008:8008 \
#        matrixdotorg/synapse:latest
#   5. docker exec synapse-live register_new_matrix_user -u alice -p test-pass-a \
#        --no-admin -c /data/homeserver.yaml http://localhost:8008
#      docker exec synapse-live register_new_matrix_user -u bob   -p test-pass-b \
#        --no-admin -c /data/homeserver.yaml http://localhost:8008
#   6. export the POSTERN_MATRIX_TEST_* env vars above, then:
#      cargo test -p postern-messenger --features matrix --test matrix_live \
#        -- --ignored --test-threads=1 --nocapture
#   (Synapse is AGPL — fine to RUN as a throwaway test service; this crate never
#    links or forks it. Any homeserver works: continuwuity, conduwuit, …)

# ── HONEST NOTE ON A FLAKE FOUND (kept, not hidden) ──
# A SECOND run against the SAME already-used Synapse accounts (without wiping
# the volume) degraded badly — the restore test ran > 13 minutes and had to be
# aborted. Cause: every prior in-memory `login_password` left a stale Matrix
# device on the alice/bob accounts whose one-time keys were exhausted, so the
# sender's Megolm key-sharing (Olm to every listed device) stalled repeatedly
# trying to claim OTKs from those dead devices. It is a TEST-HARNESS state issue
# (accumulated devices on a reused homeserver), NOT a defect in the crate: the
# clean-volume run below finished in 37.56s. Reproducible remedy: run against a
# FRESH homeserver (wipe the `synapse-data` volume + re-register) as this run
# did, or log devices out. Recorded here because the honest record includes the
# footgun, not only the green run.

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
